import "core"
import obsidian("artifacts/catln-tasks")
import obsidian("artifacts/catln-tasks/Tasks.md")


# Tests a /Data/Calendar/#calendar over a vault of tasks. A calendar shows everything the page it is
  written in reaches which sits on a day, so this page reaches the vault beside it and the calendar
  is of the tasks that vault holds.
  Written in a .ct page and not in a note. A calendar is Catln's own and not a reading of the
  Obsidian Tasks plugin, which has no such block for a note to write; what a note has to ask about
  its own vault is /Data/Note/#tasks, which is a reading of the plugin and is tested apart.
  The vault is named note by note as obsidianTasks.ct names it, and for its reason: a vault does not
  import the notes it holds, so a golden test walking out from here would reach the vault and stop.
  What the calendar finds is not written down here. The annotations of a page are evaluated for the
  file a test names, and what they evaluate to is not what a golden of the program records — so the
  annotation is what is written down here and what answers it is CalendarTests.
#calendar
main{io -> IO}= io.exit(val= 0)
